+# Walkthrough - Background Notification Reliability
+
+I have implemented changes to ensure that notifications are delivered reliably even when the app is closed or the device has been restarted.
+
+## Changes Made
+
+### 1. Enhanced Permissions
+Added critical permissions to [AndroidManifest.xml](file:///C:/Users/pawan/AndroidStudioProjects/FSSCrm/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml) to support background work:
+- `RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED`: Allows the app to resume notification polling after the phone restarts.
+- `WAKE_LOCK`: Ensures the processor doesn't go to sleep while the worker is fetching data.
+- `REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS`: Allows us to request the system to not kill the app's background tasks.
+
+### 2. Robust Scheduling
+Updated the `WorkManager` logic in [MainActivity.kt](file:///C:/Users/pawan/AndroidStudioProjects/FSSCrm/app/src/main/java/com/fsscrm/MainActivity.kt):
+- Changed policy to `ExistingPeriodicWorkPolicy.UPDATE`. This ensures that any changes to the worker configuration (like constraints or intervals) are applied immediately without waiting for the old job to expire.
+- Added linear backoff criteria so that if a network error occurs, the app retries polling in a controlled manner.
+
+### 3. Debuggable Background Worker
+Improved [NotificationWorker.kt](file:///C:/Users/pawan/AndroidStudioProjects/FSSCrm/app/src/main/java/com/fsscrm/network/NotificationWorker.kt) with comprehensive logging:
+- You can now monitor the background execution by filtering for `NotificationWorker` in Logcat.
+- It logs when it starts, what data it fetches, and if it decides to show a notification.
+
+## How to Verify
+
+### 1. Force Test via ADB
+You don't have to wait 15 minutes to test. Run this command in your terminal while the app is closed:
+```bash
+adb shell cmd jobscheduler run -f com.fsscrm 1
+```
+*(Note: `1` is the typical internal Job ID for the first WorkManager job. If it doesn't work, check `adb shell dumpsys jobscheduler` to find the correct ID.)*
+
+### 2. Monitor Logs
+Open the Logcat tab in Android Studio and search for `NotificationWorker`. You should see logs like:
+`D/NotificationWorker: Fetching dashboard data for user: 123`
+`D/NotificationWorker: Data fetched: Leads=10, Tasks=5`
+
+### 3. Battery Optimization (Crucial)
+On many Android devices (especially Xiaomi, OnePlus, Samsung), background work is still killed unless "Battery Optimization" is disabled.
+- Long press the FSS CRM app icon -> App Info -> Battery -> Select **"Unrestricted"** or **"Don't optimize"**.
+
+> [!TIP]
+> For production-grade reliability, it is highly recommended to migrate from polling to **Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM)**. Polling consumes more battery and is subject to system delays, whereas FCM is pushed directly by Google Play Services.
